deterministic tier-aware slot-backfill dispatcher for /mgh-init + /mgh-sdr
fan-out (tiers: scout/scout-merge/t1/t2/t3/sdr; zero LLM turns; fixed template
+ verbatim fields; in-flight-capped host-CLI spawn; per-unit stall kill)

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --tier {scout,scout-merge,t1,t2,t3,sdr}
                        fan-out tier (default scout). Selects the enumeration
                        script, task template, placeholder set, and fanout
                        agent; scout call shape (--scout-plan etc.) is
                        unchanged. t2 tier (map stage only): plan_aggregate.py
                        --node t2 --init-dir/[--budget]/--materialize. scout-
                        merge tier (map stage only; identical call shape):
                        plan_aggregate.py --node scout-merge --init-
                        dir/[--budget]/--materialize. sdr tier: diff_group.py
                        --repo/--base/--branch/--checkpoints/--materialize
  --scout-plan SCOUT_PLAN
                        path to scout_plan.json (scout tier; forwarded to
                        list_scout_batches)
  --clusters CLUSTERS   path to clusters.json (t1 tier; forwarded to
                        list_clusters)
  --candidates CANDIDATES
                        path to controls_candidates.json (t1 tier; hit lookup
                        for --materialize)
  --init-dir INIT_DIR   path to .mgh-init dir (t2 tier; forwarded to
                        plan_aggregate --init-dir; run_config.json under it
                        anchors the sidecar/liveness home)
  --budget BUDGET       per-request aggregate byte cap (t2 tier; forwarded to
                        plan_aggregate --budget; default None ->
                        plan_aggregate DEFAULT_BUDGET)
  --inventory INVENTORY
                        path to controls_inventory.json (t3 tier; forwarded to
                        list_rule_jobs)
  --format {opencode,claude}
                        rule format (t3 tier; determines rule_path)
  --rules-dir RULES_DIR
                        opencode rules detail dir (t3 tier; rule_path base)
  --target TARGET       target project root (t3 tier; rule_path base, default
                        .)
  --repo REPO           absolute target git repo root (sdr tier; forwarded to
                        diff_group + the tree anchor)
  --base BASE           base ref (sdr tier; default master; forwarded to
                        diff_group)
  --branch BRANCH       branch ref (sdr tier; default: current branch,
                        resolved by diff_group; forwarded verbatim)
  --checkpoints CHECKPOINTS
                        tier checkpoint dir (markers; forwarded to the tier's
                        list script). REQUIRED for dispatch; OPTIONAL for
                        --kill-stale recovery (scan hint only — the run dir is
                        then anchored by the tier's plan artifact, e.g.
                        --scout-plan/--init-dir/--repo)
  --inputs-dir INPUTS_DIR
                        per-unit input dir (forwarded as --materialize; audit
                        <unit>.task.md copies land here). REQUIRED for
                        dispatch; OPTIONAL for --kill-stale recovery (scan
                        hint only)
  --host {claude,opencode,dsh}
                        explicit host CLI; default: opencode in PATH, else
                        claude, else dsh, else exit 2 + manual-dispatch
                        fallback recipe
  --wave WAVE           in-flight subprocess cap (default 5) - a slot-backfill
                        concurrency limit, not a batch size: any unit reaching
                        a terminal state frees its slot and the next queued
                        unit is dispatched immediately
  --time-budget-ms TIME_BUDGET_MS
                        soft deadline in ms: stop dispatching new units, drain
                        in-flight, exit 0 with partial:true (re-dispatch the
                        same command). RECOMMENDED = host per-call timeout x
                        0.8 (host 900000ms -> 720000; claude Bash cap 600000ms
                        -> 480000); MUST stay BELOW the host per-call timeout
                        (soft deadline fires before the host hard kill; four-
                        level invariant: stall-timeout-s < call-timeout-s <
                        time-budget-ms x 0.8 < host per-call timeout, >=20%
                        per level — the innermost level is dropped when
                        --stall-timeout-s 0). When passed, --call-timeout-s
                        MUST be passed explicitly and stay below budget x 0.8
                        (spawn-time validation, exit 2 otherwise)
  --call-timeout-s CALL_TIMEOUT_S
                        per-subprocess absolute timeout in seconds (default
                        7200 for out-of-host manual runs; calibrated for slow
                        intranet LLM endpoints, ~4x headroom; better-slow-
                        than-killed: a killed unit leaves no marker, stays
                        pending, re-dispatch wastes a whole run). When --time-
                        budget-ms is passed this flag MUST be passed
                        explicitly and stay below budget x 0.8. The kill path
                        is the whole-tree kill (see --stall-timeout-s)
  --stall-timeout-s STALL_TIMEOUT_S
                        per-unit output-silence window in seconds, INITIAL
                        value. FOUR-segment value domain: 0 = disable the
                        criterion outright; 1..59 = rejected with exit 2 (this
                        is the HARD REJECTION FLOOR = 60); 60.. = a valid
                        silence window. The default 900 is the OUT-OF-HOST
                        MANUAL-RUN default ONLY - it is NEVER a rejection
                        floor and MUST NOT be described as one (the floor is
                        60). When --time-budget-ms is passed (a host-driven
                        run) this flag MUST be passed explicitly and stay
                        below --call-timeout-s (the same four-level invariant)
                        - NEVER omit it and rely on the default 900: the host-
                        driven --call-timeout-s is far smaller, so the stall <
                        call spawn-time check would refuse to start with exit
                        2 and make the tier unusable. Recommended host-driven
                        value: 300. Semantics: no stdout/stderr bytes for this
                        long -> that unit's whole process tree is killed
                        (taskkill /T /F - the .cmd shim chain's real host-CLI
                        process dies too) and the unit stays pending for re-
                        dispatch. Byte-silence means the unit produced NOTHING
                        for that long; it does NOT mean the unit is hung - on
                        a rate-limited gateway the usual cause is the call
                        waiting in the gateway's queue, which is locally
                        indistinguishable from a real hang (same silence, same
                        zero output). The criterion's job is to bound how long
                        one slot may sit idle in this run - a convergence
                        bound - never to diagnose unit health. Silence means
                        BOTH streams idle (the clock takes the newer of the
                        two streams' last-byte stamps), so a unit whose stderr
                        never emits is not mistaken for silent. Default
                        calibration: p99(completed-unit runtime) x 2, rounded
                        down to the minute and never below 900 (observed
                        sample n=84: p50 134s / p95 229s / p99 325s / max 325s
                        -> 650s -> 600s -> floor 900s). That sample is the
                        SURVIVING population only - units killed for gateway
                        queueing are absent from it by construction - so it
                        bounds healthy units and MUST NOT be read as 'past
                        this it deserves to die'. Re-calibrate from the
                        runtime_* fields every exit reports. The EFFECTIVE
                        window widens within a run on false-kill self-
                        evidence: if a stall-killed unit then completes on re-
                        dispatch in the same run, the window becomes min(2x
                        current, --call-timeout-s x 0.8) for the rest of the
                        run (stderr-disclosed, once per triggering unit). 0
                        disables the criterion outright - convergence is then
                        bounded by --call-timeout-s ALONE, so a host-driven
                        run SHOULD tighten that value; the 'stall < call'
                        ordering constraint does not apply in this mode. A
                        mis-killed unit re-dispatches and its
                        <checkpoints>/<tier>/<unit>.run.log keeps the
                        evidence, but it also loses the work already done AND
                        its queue position. Kill scope is the unit's own
                        session (units are spawned with start_new_session) -
                        it NEVER terminates the dispatcher, and an interactive
                        Ctrl-C no longer reaches in-flight units (the exit
                        path tree-kills them; a hard kill's orphans are
                        cleared with --kill-stale)
  --hb-interval-s HB_INTERVAL_S
                        stderr in-flight heartbeat period in seconds (default
                        60): one line per running unit (unit id + seconds
                        since its child's last output) so a human
                        distinguishes 'slow' from 'hung' live from the host
                        TUI; stdout stays a single JSON line
  --stall-waves STALL_WAVES
                        convergence circuit breaker window multiplier: every
                        --stall-waves x --wave dispatched units the disk
                        terminal count (done+failed markers) is re-derived
                        once; --stall-waves consecutive zero-growth re-
                        derivations with units still queued/in-flight -> stop
                        dispatching, exit 2 (default 2; stdout carries
                        stalled:true + stalled_pending[] with each stuck
                        unit's marker existence; a stall-killed unit does not
                        raise the disk count, so a deterministic hang is
                        truncated here; recipe: stop re-dispatching, diagnose
                        via resume_state.py --check)
  --resume              re-derive pending from disk markers (same entry point
                        as a fresh call; kept for call-shape parity)
  --cooldown-s COOLDOWN_S
                        fast-fail storm cooldown in seconds (default 300; 0 =
                        off): when >= 3 requeue events
                        (crash/timeout/stall/spawn-error units returning to
                        the queue tail; failed acks and pre-spawn anchor
                        failures NEVER count — they terminate without
                        requeueing) accumulate within a 120s sliding window,
                        new dispatch pauses for this many seconds while in-
                        flight units keep running and harvesting. The wait is
                        capped by the remaining --time-budget-ms minus an in-
                        flight convergence margin (observed avg unit duration
                        x in-flight count); when the budget cannot cover it
                        the cooldown degrades to continuing immediately with a
                        stderr disclosure. Also arms the pre-breaker backoff:
                        when the zero-progress breaker is about to exit 2, one
                        single 'cooldown -> full disk re-derivation -> re-
                        observe' round runs first (disk growth disarms the
                        breaker; still zero growth exits 2 stalled) — never
                        more than once per call. 0 disables the cooldown AND
                        the pre-breaker backoff. stdout summary carries
                        cooldowns:<n>
  --no-rate-limit-stop  disable the rate-limit crash-storm fast-path
                        truncation (default: ON). With it on, every crashed
                        unit's output tails are classified against quota
                        signatures (429 / too many requests / rate limit /
                        quota, case-insensitive, crash terminal states only);
                        when >= --wave crashes since the last disk terminal
                        advance are ALL rate-limit, dispatch stops immediately
                        (fast path, ahead of any new cooldown) and the runner
                        exits 2 with stdout rate_limited:true +
                        rate_limited_crashes:[ids] and a stderr wait-one-
                        quota-window recipe. Signature drift (all crashes
                        classify unknown) degrades gracefully: no truncation,
                        the event-driven cooldown and the breaker still
                        backstop. Non-rate-limit crashes never count toward
                        the threshold
  --retry-failed        re-dispatch units the tier enumerator re-lists as
                        failed (it is invoked with --include-failed when this
                        flag is set — unit identity ALWAYS comes from the
                        enumerator, never from filename stems). At claim the
                        unit's .failed marker is deleted (the failure evidence
                        stays in the unit's *.run.log) and the unit dispatches
                        normally; a re-failure writes a fresh marker
                        (naturally bounded — failed acks never requeue) and
                        each unit is retried at most once per call. stdout
                        summary carries retried_failed:<n>. Orchestrator
                        discipline: at most one --retry-failed round per tier
                        wrap-up (failed>0 with provider-transient run.log
                        shape), then accept the gap and disclose it in the
                        report
  --kill-stale          orphan-tree cleanup: inspect <init-
                        dir>/fanout_runner.<tier>.pid liveness files and kill
                        what they record (runner tree; or, when the runner is
                        dead, recorded host-CLI child trees — matched by the
                        EXECUTABLE TOKEN (argv[0] basename, .exe/.cmd/.bat
                        stripped, never a whole-cmdline substring match, so an
                        unrelated process merely mentioning a host name in its
                        arguments is never killed)). Needed after a HARD kill
                        only: units lead their own session, so an interactive
                        Ctrl-C no longer reaches in-flight units through the
                        tty, and the dispatcher's exit path tree-kills
                        whatever is still registered before deregistering.
                        DESTRUCTIVE: run with --dry-run first - a real kill
                        with detected targets and no prior dry-run review
                        exits 2 + recipe. Idempotent; tier-agnostic
  --pending-file <list-stdout.json>
                        TEST HOOK: consume the tier listing from a file (no
                        spawn, no list invocation)
  --purge-audit         delete <inputs-dir>/*.task.md audit copies (with
                        --dry-run: list only)
  --dry-run             with --purge-audit: list without deleting; alone: skip
                        dispatch
  --template <tier-task.md>
                        override task-message template path (default: sibling
                        prompts/fragments/fanout/<tier>-task.md of this
                        install)
